gojja2 is an independent, clean-room-style reimplementation of the Jinja2
template language in Go. No source code from the projects below is copied into
this repository. They are consulted as behavioural references and used as test
inputs at development time only: `make suites` downloads them into
./third_party/, which is excluded from version control, and `make import` turns
their templates into conformance cases under ./testdata/generated/, which is
also excluded. Nothing from any of them is vendored, committed or
redistributed.

Only their *inputs* are used. Every expected output is regenerated by rendering
with the pinned CPython jinja2, because that is this project's specification --
so where an upstream disagrees with CPython, the upstream's own answer is
discarded rather than encoded here.

The oracle
----------

  * Jinja (https://github.com/pallets/jinja) - BSD-3-Clause.
    The CPython implementation is the ground-truth oracle: expected output for
    every conformance corpus is produced by rendering templates with the real
    `jinja2` package, pinned to 3.1.6. Jinja's own pytest suite is additionally
    harvested as a source of behavioural cases.

Other implementations of the language, consulted for breadth
------------------------------------------------------------

  * MiniJinja (https://github.com/mitsuhiko/minijinja) - Apache-2.0.
    Its template fixture corpus is consulted as a source of behavioural cases.

  * minja (https://github.com/google/minja) - MIT.
    Its syntax tests are consulted as a source of behavioural cases.

  * llama.cpp (https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp) - MIT.
    Its Jinja tests, and the model chat templates vendored alongside them, are
    consulted as a source of behavioural cases.

Real-world templates, consulted as corpora
------------------------------------------

  * chat_templates (https://github.com/chujiezheng/chat_templates) - MIT.
    A collection of LLM chat templates. Individual model templates carry their
    own upstream licences, which range from Apache-2.0 to bespoke terms; none
    is redistributed here.

  * Material for MkDocs (https://github.com/squidfunk/mkdocs-material) - MIT.
    Its theme templates are consulted for inheritance, partials and blocks,
    which the chat-template corpora do not exercise.

  * cookiecutter-django
    (https://github.com/cookiecutter/cookiecutter-django) - BSD-3-Clause.
  * cookiecutter-pypackage
    (https://github.com/audreyfeldroy/cookiecutter-pypackage) - MIT.
  * cookiecutter-data-science
    (https://github.com/drivendata/cookiecutter-data-science) - MIT.
  * cookiecutter-hypermodern-python
    (https://github.com/cjolowicz/cookiecutter-hypermodern-python) - MIT.
    Four project generators, consulted for templates that generate a file
    rather than a page: `{% raw %}` blocks and whitespace control.

The revision each corpus was built from, and its licence, is recorded again
next to the generated cases in testdata/generated/*/SOURCES.md. The pins
themselves live in the Makefile.
